    imageAlt: "Dispersed bombers on an East Anglian field",
    dek: "On February 3, eight aircraft of the 95th do not come home. Frank Calicura has been at Horham a little under a month. He is not on the board. He is there.",
    sections: [
      {
        id: "8.1",
        title: "The Order",
        blocks: [
          { type: "p", text: "On the morning of February 3, 1945, one of the largest American bombing missions of the war took off from airfields across East Anglia, bound for Berlin." },
          { type: "p", text: "Just over a thousand B-17s were dispatched, escorted by long-range fighters that finally, by this stage of the war, could accompany them the entire distance. The order named the Friedrichstrasse railway station as the aiming point — a target in the heart of the city, chosen deliberately for its location. General Jimmy Doolittle, commanding the Eighth Air Force, objected when he received it. To him, an aiming point that close to the city center amounted to bombing Berlin itself rather than a military objective. He raised the objection to his superior, General Carl Spaatz, and was overruled. The mission flew as ordered." },
          { type: "p", text: "The bomber stream stretched some 360 miles and took roughly ninety minutes to pass over the city. Berlin’s defenses — forty miles of searchlights, flak batteries, and decoy fires, still backed by enough fighter aircraft to make the approach dangerous even this late in the war — met the formation in full. A lieutenant colonel leading the 91st Bomb Group over the target that day did not come home. Neither did eight aircraft of the 95th Bomb Group." },
          { type: "p", text: "Frank Calicura’s group. Frank Calicura’s base." },
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "8.2",
        title: "The Eleventh Day",
        blocks: [
          { type: "p", text: "He had been at Horham a little under a month." },
          { type: "p", text: "Frank's Certificate of Service places his arrival in England on January 6, 1945, not the first of the month this book had previously assumed without a document behind it. His qualification record gives a formal assignment date to the 335th Bomb Squadron, 95th Bomb Group, of January 11, 1945 — five days into the process described here, not the ten this book had calculated from the earlier, unsourced date — orientation, local flying, the accumulation of currency in the aircraft and the theater before a new crew was trusted with a combat assignment. He was not on the February 3 mission, and no record suggests his crew was ever slated for it. New crews simply were not yet in rotation for a raid of that scale and risk, and the absence of his name from that day’s loss list is not a near miss to be dramatized — it is the ordinary consequence of being new." },
          { type: "p", text: "Frank's own Individual Flight Record survives for this exact window, and it turns \"orientation, local flying, the accumulation of currency\" into specific numbers. A supplemental entry for December 1944 logs a single B-17G flight, 5 hours 10 minutes as first pilot, with an hour of instrument time. January 1945 adds three more B-17G flights before combat began — 8 hours 30 minutes, 5 hours 25 minutes, and 1 hour 30 minutes as first pilot, 15 hours 25 minutes in all — alongside three one-hour sessions in the Link trainer, the ground-based instrument simulator every crew used to keep instrument skills current between actual flights. The record is certified by Robert J. Lynch, Captain, Air Corps, Assistant Operations Officer, 95th Bomb Group (family collection)." },
          { type: "p", text: "The same document gives a transfer date to the 335th Bomb Squadron of January 14, 1945 — three days later than the January 11 date already established here from the qualification record. The two documents do not explain the difference." },
          { type: "p", text: "But he was there. Whatever the station felt like on the evening of February 3, with eight crews’ worth of empty bunks and gear still hanging where men had left it that morning, Frank Calicura felt it too, from inside the same huts, eleven days before he would fly his own first mission from the same airfield." },
          { type: "p", text: "No letter, no clipping, and no document in the family’s collection records what that evening was like, or what Frank made of it. The silence itself is worth stating plainly rather than filled in." },
        ],
      },
      {
        id: "8.3",
        title: "What the Board Would Ask",
        blocks: [
          { type: "p", text: "Missions like February 3 were not routine, but the machinery that produced them was." },
          { type: "p", text: "Every operational day at Horham began with the same sequence: a board posted before dawn naming which squadrons and crews would fly, briefings that revealed the target only once the crews were assembled, the long wait through assembly and the Channel crossing, and eventually a debriefing hut that either filled with the right number of men or did not. For eleven days after February 3, Frank Calicura was not yet part of that sequence. He was watching it happen to other crews, from the outside, in a station that had just taken its heaviest loss in recent memory and would go on flying missions the next day, and the day after that, because that was what the schedule required regardless of what the debriefing hut had looked like the night before." },
          { type: "p", text: "On the morning of February 14, a name went up on the board that had not been there before. It was his." },
        ],
      },
    ],
